The order is the following:

- `targetTLS` `'end'` event
- `targetTLS` `'error'` event (we are interested in that and want to write to the `internalTLS` there but the problem is that `internalTLS` gets closed in the previous `'end'` event.)

 `createTLSSocket` is only used for client certificates. Its internally using `createConnectionAsync` which we don't want to modify.  Changing `createTLSSocket` seems reasonable to me - wdyt? I didn't want to pollute our existing server fixture with more specific options which are only relevant for this test, hence creating a pure TLS server here. Listening to `'close'` seems better, changed it to `'close'`. For close the order is end, error, close. Sounds good. - I think this line is no-op, because we add a different function as an even listener.
- The comment did not really help me understand why do we need to remove the listener. It's save to not listen on `targetTLS.once('close` here since we already to it in line 94. - Can it close before either `secureConnect` or `error` happen?
- I see we remove the listener in line 160 (new code). Who is going to close `internalTLS` in this case? ```suggestion
        if (this._closed) {
          internalTLS.destroy();
          return;
        }
```

